    ],
    "note": "House is the building: a house with three bedrooms, a house for sale. Home is the place where you live, whatever kind of building it is, and it carries the feeling of belonging: a happy home, there's no place like home. You buy a house and make it a home. People selling houses often say home because it sounds warmer: new homes for sale."
  },
  "usage_note": "You go home, get home, stay home (American) or stay at home, and are at home: no the, and in American English often no preposition. With house you need a determiner and a preposition: go to my house, at Sara's house. Move house is British; Americans say move.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "I went to house at six.",
      "correct": "I went home at six.",
      "note": "Your own place is home, used without to or the; house is the building."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "I stayed in house all day because of the rain.",
      "correct": "I stayed home all day because of the rain.",
      "note": "Stay home (American) or stay at home; in the house is used only when the contrast is with outside."
    }
  ],
